  "narrative": {
    "intro": "RTX has $4B in federal contract obligations for FY2026 to date across 822 awards, with an average award size of $4.8M and obligations down 45.6% year over year. The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer, obligating $4B. Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing is the largest market, with $1.7B obligated, followed by Other Aircraft Parts and Auxiliary Equipment Manufacturing at $819M and Aircraft Manufacturing at $805M. Historical obligations show variability: FY21 had $3.7B, FY22 had $8B, FY23 had $8.2B, FY24 had $5B, and FY25 had $7.3B. Smaller agency activity includes the National Aeronautics and Space Administration at $1.2M and the Department of Homeland Security at $233K. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences accounts for $42.9M, Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing accounts for $14.1M, and several smaller NAICS lines together account for the remaining obligations. Current-year totals are partial because figures are fiscal year to date. This snapshot shows where federal dollars hit RTX during the current fiscal period and which product and service lines received the largest obligations.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who are RTX's biggest federal buyers?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $4B. The National Aeronautics and Space Administration obligated $1.2M and the Department of Homeland Security obligated $233K. Other agencies did not appear in the top buying list for FY2026 to date."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What are RTX's largest product and service markets for federal work?",
        "body": "Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing accounted for $1.7B in obligations. Other Aircraft Parts and Auxiliary Equipment Manufacturing accounted for $819M and Aircraft Manufacturing accounted for $805M. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences accounted for $42.9M and Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing accounted for $14.1M."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much does RTX make from federal contracts?",
      "a": "RTX makes $4B from federal contracts in FY2026 to date. RTX has 822 awards this fiscal year to date with an average award size of $4.8M, and obligations are down 45.6% year over year. Historical context shows FY25 obligations were $7.3B and FY23 obligations were $8.2B."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which agencies buy from RTX?",
      "a": "The Department of Defense is the primary buyer, obligating $4B. The National Aeronautics and Space Administration bought $1.2M and the Department of Homeland Security bought $233K in FY2026 to date."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does RTX sell to the government?",
      "a": "RTX sells aircraft-related manufacturing and related services to the government, led by Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing with $1.7B in obligations. Other significant products and services include Other Aircraft Parts and Auxiliary Equipment Manufacturing at $819M, Aircraft Manufacturing at $805M, and Other Support Activities for Air Transportation at $552M. RTX also has obligations in Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences at $42.9M and Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing at $14.1M."
    },
